-- §9 casual strings: a "..." literal is parse-time sugar for a
-- cons-list of codepoint ints — the same shape the §11.5 list literal
-- builds ([104, 105] IS "hi"). No Char, no Str runtime type (§11.4,
-- provisionally resolved): plain Ints all the way down, and
-- interpretation is up to whichever action consumes the value (§3.3).
-- say %s decodes a codepoint list; atomize/atos convert to/from atoms
-- (atomize fatally errors on an uncapitalized one, §4).

{ ("greet", "world") }

-- the literal in this pattern desugars exactly like the one in the
-- initial tuple above, so they match structurally
("greet", who) : [say "hello, %s" who; (Next,)]

-- atos turns an atom into a casual string; %s consumes any codepoint
-- list, escapes and non-ASCII included
(Next,) : [say "%s\n%s" atos(Bye) "ciao ☺"; exit 0]
